course: dictionary
lesson: 3403
title: Explanation: what it means and why it matters
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: An explanation is something that makes a thing clear, or the reason something happened. «A definition is a short explanation of what a word means.» Here the sentence tells you that an explanation helps you understand what a word means.
explain: The second meaning is a reason. «Is there an explanation for why you are late?» The words "why you are late" are the clue: an explanation here is the reason for being late.
explain: Good explanations often use small joining words such as «because», «so» and «therefore». «The pond froze, so the ducks walked on the ice.» The word «so» links the cause, the frozen pond, to the result, the ducks walking on the ice.
explain: When you have done something wrong, an honest explanation is better than excuses. Say what really happened, and say sorry if you need to.
fact: An explanation makes something clear, or gives the reason something happened.
fact: «Explanation» is a noun, and its plural is «explanations».
fact: A clear explanation answers the question how or why.
fact: In maths, show your working as an explanation, not just the answer.
fact: Useful phrases: give an explanation, a clear explanation, explanation of, explanation for, no explanation.
exercise: «A definition is a short explanation of what a word means.» What is an explanation in this sentence? | A score on a test | *Something that makes a meaning clear and easy to understand | A fast animal that lives in the sea | A drum roll before something is announced
exercise: «Is there an explanation for why you are late?» What does explanation mean here? | A loud noise in the playground | A map of the town | *A reason for something that happened | A spelling mistake
exercise: «The pond froze, so the ducks walked on the ice.» Which word joins the cause to the result? | ducks | pond | walked | *so
test: «Before believing or sharing, ask: is there another explanation?» In this sentence, another explanation means | a rule about sharing toys | a fish that lives in a pond | *a different reason or way to make sense of it
test: Which word completes the sentence? There is no ___ for the broken window. | explains | *explanation | explanatory | explain
exam: Which sentence gives a good explanation for an empty lunchbox? | Dogs are very good at swimming | *I forgot to pack my sandwiches, so the box was empty | The sun is hot today | Lunchboxes are usually blue
